  "final_message": "I haven't removed anything, because the premise doesn't hold: `analytics-ingest` reads `channel` from `OrderPlaced`.\n\n- `analytics-ingest/src/core/events.ts:34` parses the event with `reader.oneOf(\"channel\", CHANNELS)`.\n- `analytics-ingest/src/ingest.ts:17` copies it into the `orders.channel` column.\n- `analytics-ingest/README.md:27-31` lists `channel` as part of the `OrderPlaced` contract. A missing or unknown `channel` makes ingestion reject the whole event with a `ContractViolationError`.\n\nIf I dropped `channel` from the event, every new `OrderPlaced` would be rejected by analytics, and its order reporting would stop. `billing-service` only mentions `channel` in test fixtures, so it isn't affected.\n\nWithin orders-service, `channel` is used in `core/order.ts` (the order model and validation), `core/events.ts`, `api/requestParsing.ts`, and several tests. The `POST /orders` example in the README and CLAUDE.md also includes it. Removing it from the order model would also stop `POST /orders` from requiring it.\n\nWhich of these do you want?\n1. Coordinate with the analytics team first. They would make `channel` optional or remove it, and then I'd remove it here.\n2. Remove it from the order model and request parsing only, and keep emitting `channel` on the event. That isn't possible without storing it somewhere, so it probably isn't what you want.\n3. Go ahead anyway, accepting that analytics ingestion breaks until they change.\n\nI'd go with option 1.",
